/** @page xmlreleasenotes Release Notes

    @tableofcontents

    @section xml230 xml Module Version 2.3.0

    This release makes the module's XML, XML Schema, SOAP and WebDAV processing conform to the standards they
    implement; see @ref xmlsoapguide, @ref xmlwebdavguide and @ref xmlschemavalidation for the supported standards.
    The changes of the user modules are listed in their own release notes (see @ref xml230usermodules).

    Release builds now report XML reader namespace errors through the same exceptions as debug builds;
    parser diagnostic suppression no longer disables the reader's error callback. The bundled
    \c webdav-server utility uses the current WebDavHandler API and shuts down cleanly on SIGTERM.

    @subsection xml230compat Compatibility Notes
    - %Qore 3.0 or later is required, with a C++17 compiler; building with CMake requires CMake 3.18 or later
    - internal parsed entities are now expanded (XML 1.0 section 4.4.3); a reference such as <tt>&amp;who;</tt> to
      <tt>&lt;!ENTITY who "world"&gt;</tt> was silently dropped by every parser. A reference to an external entity
      now fails the parse with an error naming the entity's URI, where it was silently dropped before; external
      entities are never loaded. With a libxml2 older than 2.14, entity references are not expanded. See
      @ref xmlentityreferences
    - XML data parsing retains whitespace-only scalar values, which were returned as empty values or
      @ref nothing "NOTHING", and honors inherited \c xml:space attributes
    - \c http:// and \c https:// schema resources are retrieved with %Qore's HTTP client; HTTPS verifies the server
      certificate and host name against %Qore's OpenSSL trust store, so schemas served with an untrusted
      certificate are no longer loaded
    - schema resource, catalog and redirect loads are checked against the calling %Program's sandbox policy
      (\c ILLEGAL-FILESYSTEM-ACCESS, \c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED, \c ILLEGAL-NETWORK-ACCESS,
      \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED); see @ref xmlschemavalidationresources
    - the native \c XmlRpcClient class is deprecated in favor of the new @ref XmlRpcClientIo::XmlRpcClientIo
      "XmlRpcClientIo" user module
    - SOAP messages must supply every header that their WSDL binding describes (WS-I Basic Profile R2738): a bound
      header without a value now raises \c SOAP-SERIALIZATION-ERROR, where it was silently left out of the message;
      a nillable element part without a value is now serialized as nil instead of being rejected (see the
      @ref wsdlreleasenotes "WSDL release notes")

    @subsection xml230new New Features
    - added @ref Qore::Xml::XPF_PRESERVE_WHITESPACE "XPF_PRESERVE_WHITESPACE" to retain all character whitespace
      inside elements with @ref Qore::Xml::parse_xml() "parse_xml()", the schema-validating parsers, @ref
      Qore::Xml::XmlDoc "XmlDoc" and
      @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader "XmlReader"; see @ref xmlwhitespaceserialization
    - added XML element fragments: a \c ^xml^ key embeds the document element of a complete XML document when
      generating XML, preserving its lexical content, namespaces and node order; see @ref xmlelementfragments
    - added @ref Qore::Xml::XmlDoc::validateSchemaFile() "XmlDoc::validateSchemaFile()" for schemas that span
      several files; relative \c xsd:import and \c xsd:include locations resolve against the schema file
    - added @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader::schemaValidateString() "XmlReader::schemaValidateString()" to attach an XSD
      given as text to a reader before reading; @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader::schemaValidate() "schemaValidate()"
      takes a schema file or URI, and raises \c XMLREADER-XSD-ERROR for a reader created from an
      @ref Qore::Xml::XmlDoc "XmlDoc" or after reading has started
    - added @ref Qore::Xml::normalize_xsd_uri() "normalize_xsd_uri()" to validate an XSD \c anyURI value and return
      its whitespace-normalized spelling
    - added @ref Qore::Xml::convert_xsd_float() "convert_xsd_float()" for strict XSD \c float and \c double
      conversion, with nearest rounding to
      binary32 or binary64, signed zero, subnormals and the special values, independent of the locale and the C
      library
    - added @ref Qore::Xml::canonical_xsd_float() "canonical_xsd_float()" to return the canonical spelling of an XSD
      \c float or \c double value
    - @ref Qore::Xml::AbstractXmlIoInputCallback::open() "AbstractXmlIoInputCallback::open()" is called before
      any other loader: a callback serving schemas from memory needs no filesystem or network access, returning
      @ref nothing "NOTHING" falls back to the checked loader, and an exception from the callback or its stream
      propagates to the caller; each open resource has its own stream, so nested schema compilation and
      cancellation work

    @subsection xml230xml XML Parsing and Generation
    - text and CDATA generation writes carriage returns as character references, and attribute generation writes
      tabs, line feeds and carriage returns as character references, so their values survive XML line-ending and
      attribute normalization; formatting no longer adds character data around CDATA or inside preserved-space
      content
    - parsing retains ordered mixed text, and repeated-child grouping when element-only indentation is omitted
    - fixed XML string readers decoding already converted UTF-8 text again according to the XML declaration;
      string parsing honors the string's %Qore encoding and rejects embedded NUL characters
    - fixed UTF-16 document and fragment output by converting the complete generated markup
    - fixed serializing hash and list members holding a weak (\c ":=") or opaque (\c "@=") reference, which were
      rejected with \c "cannot serialize type 'weak hash reference'"
    - @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader "XmlReader" cursor conversion returns scalar text and empty values, stops at the
      current element boundary and preserves the following siblings; discarded results still advance the reader
    - XML conversion releases partially built values when a schema callback reports an error
    - XML generation supports cooperative cancellation and keeps its original exception on failure

    @subsection xml230xsd XML Schema Validation
    Validation follows XML Schema 1.0; the private libxml2 build (see @ref xml230build) corrects the following
    defects of the upstream releases, and the configuration probes reject a system libxml2 that has them:
    - identity constraints: unresolved \c IDREF and \c IDREFS values and IDs identifying distinct elements are
      rejected, including list and union values, defaults, forward references and wildcards; \c xs:key,
      \c xs:unique and \c xs:keyref tables keep distinct keys after sibling conflicts, treat nil as a missing
      value, reject nillable key fields and compare list, atomic, QName and NOTATION values correctly
    - particles and occurrence: unique particle attribution is checked with exact counted repetitions before
      automaton reduction; finite occurrence bounds keep their exact values, also above native integer ranges;
      nested finite repetitions keep every counter increment; distinct particle positions are kept for local and
      global elements, shared group uses, \c xs:all members and wildcards; occurrence attributes accept signed
      nonnegative integers and whitespace, and elements with <tt>maxOccurs="0"</tt> contribute no particle
    - declarations: explicit and implicit element declarations must be consistent; anonymous simple types inherit
      \c finalDefault; substitution groups respect blocked derivations and abstract heads; contradictory length
      facets are rejected; \c NOTATION declarations and types are checked; empty group references keep their
      content when extending mixed types
    - values: \c ENTITY and \c ENTITIES values, the minimum length of \c ENTITIES, \c IDREFS and \c NMTOKENS,
      ordered union matching (also after a QName member rejects an unbound prefix), QName namespace identity
      (the implicit \c xml prefix, and absent and empty namespaces), unsigned builtins rejecting signed lexical
      forms, \c anyURI rejecting bare schemes, timezone-normalized time comparisons, exact calendar values
      with fractional seconds and extended years, \c float and \c double exponents, and typed comparison of
      fixed values
    - the \c Name, \c NCName, \c QName and \c NMTOKEN types, and the types derived from them, use the name
      characters of XML 1.0 Second Edition that XSD 1.0 requires; libxml2 2.15 accepted XML 1.0 Fifth Edition
      characters such as U+0370 or U+10000. XML element and attribute names keep the Fifth Edition characters
    - default and fixed values: canonical spellings of numeric, boolean, calendar and binary values are checked
      under the actual type, including list and union members; empty elements receive their defaults under
      their actual type
    - character content: empty CDATA counts as no characters, whitespace CDATA is allowed in element-only
      content and schema declarations, and annotation text and markup are preserved
    - wildcards: strict element wildcards assess an available \c xsi:type, and wildcard ID conflicts are reported
    - schema documents: foreign attributes on annotations, identity-constraint selectors and fields, and
      component QName references with surrounding whitespace are handled as XSD 1.0 requires
    - schema locations: \c xsd:include, \c xsd:import and \c xsd:redefine locations accept raw Unicode characters
      and spaces while preserving percent escapes and XML Base; relative locations resolve against the final URI
      after HTTP redirects; runtime schema hints keep complete \c anyURI values and document bases
    - allocation failures during validation are reported as internal validation errors with complete cleanup
    - a schema import callback exception no longer produces a misleading skipped-import warning

    @subsection xml230xmlrpc XML-RPC
    - strings and struct names retain whitespace, carriage returns, empty names and adjacent text and CDATA; comments
      and processing instructions do not split a value
    - generation supports UTF-16 output and rejects characters that XML 1.0 forbids; signed 32-bit minimum values
      keep the \c i4 wire type, and the deprecated encoding-specific functions keep their arguments
    - empty values preserve the following collection members, and duplicate member names release replaced values

    @subsection xml230webdav WebDAV
    The @ref webdavhandlerintro "WebDavHandler" server now meets
    <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4918">RFC 4918</a> compliance classes 1 and 2 and passes all five
    suites of the <a href="https://notroj.github.io/litmus/">litmus</a> WebDAV compliance test, which runs in CI;
    see @ref xmlwebdavguide "WebDAV Servers and Clients":
    - write locks are enforced: a request that changes a locked resource, a resource below a depth-infinity lock or
      a locked member of a collection requires the lock token (<tt>423 Locked</tt> otherwise), and \c If headers
      are evaluated for every method (RFC 4918 section 10.4; <tt>412 Precondition Failed</tt> when false)
    - \c COPY with <tt>Depth: 0</tt> copies a collection without its members, \c MOVE accepts
      <tt>Depth: infinity</tt>, both return 201 or 204 as RFC 4918 section 9.8.5 requires, and copying onto the root
      collection is refused
    - \c LOCK accepts the empty \c exclusive, \c shared and \c write elements; locks are refreshed and removed
      anywhere in their scope; lock discovery and \c PROPPATCH responses give request URLs instead of server paths
    - entity tags are quoted strings derived from the file's inode, size and modification time
    - \c COPY and \c MOVE carry dead properties to the destination and replace those of an overwritten resource
    - streamed \c PUT and \c POST request bodies work over HTTP/2, and oversized or stalled bodies get
      <tt>413</tt> or <tt>408</tt> responses
    New WebDAV clients and a WebDAV data provider are listed in @ref xml230usermodules.

    @subsection xml230build Build and Dependencies
    - CMake probes the behavior of the installed libxml2 (namespace URI decoding, XSD QName, ENTITY and schema
      resource URIs, and the XML Schema conformance checks listed above); the \c QORE_XML_LIBXML2_PROVIDER option
      selects \c AUTO (the default: the system library if it passes, otherwise a private build), \c SYSTEM or
      \c BUNDLED
    - the private build uses libxml2 2.15.4, pinned by URL and SHA-256 and fetched at configuration time (or given
      with \c FETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_QORE_XML_LIBXML2 for offline builds), with checked corrections applied in the
      build tree, including the upstream fix passing the validation context to the SAX entity handler (libxml2
      \c 91586dc6); it is linked statically with hidden symbols, does not replace a system libxml2, and installs
      its license notices under \c share/licenses/qore-xml
    - the private build parses IEEE floating-point values with the vendored fast_float v8.3.0 (MIT license),
      preserving nearest-even conversion and the caller's floating-point state, and keeps the libxml2 source
      root out of the C++ header search path (its \c VERSION file shadowed \c &lt;version&gt; on case-insensitive
      file systems)
    - CMake policies are enabled through 3.31 for a clean configuration with CMake 4
    - the autotools build does not probe or build libxml2; use CMake for the private libxml2

    @subsection xml230usermodules User Modules
    Each user module's release notes describe its changes in detail; follow the version links.

    New user modules:
    - @ref soapclientiointro "SoapClientIo" @ref soapclientioreleasenotes "1.0": an asynchronous I/O SOAP client with
      HTTP/3, HTTP/2 and HTTP/1.1 negotiation and
      connection pooling; the recommended SOAP client for new code
    - @ref xmlrpcclientiointro "XmlRpcClientIo" @ref xmlrpcclientioreleasenotes "1.0": an asynchronous I/O XML-RPC
      client, the recommended replacement for the native
      @ref Qore::Xml::XmlRpcClient "XmlRpcClient" class
    - @ref webdavclientintro "WebDavClient" @ref webdavclientreleasenotes "1.2": a WebDAV client and connection
      provider using the synchronous \c HTTPClient API
    - @ref webdavclientiointro "WebDavClientIo" @ref webdavclientioreleasenotes "1.0": an asynchronous I/O WebDAV
      client; the recommended WebDAV client for new code
    - @ref webdavclientdataproviderintro "WebDavClientDataProvider" @ref webdavclientdataproviderreleasenotes "1.1":
      data provider actions for WebDAV resources: list, get, put, delete, mkdir,
      copy and move
    - @ref cargoxmldataproviderintro "CargoXmlDataProvider" @ref cargoxmldataproviderreleasenotes "1.0": validates,
      parses and builds XSD-defined cargo messages (UN/CEFACT
      Cross-Industry schemas included; IATA Cargo-XML)
    - @ref cdadataproviderintro "CdaDataProvider" @ref cdadataproviderreleasenotes "1.0": parses and validates HL7
      CDA / HL7 v3 documents through the data provider API

    Updated user modules:
    - @ref wsdlintro "WSDL" @ref wsdlreleasenotes "0.5.8": WSDL 1.1, SOAP 1.1 and SOAP 1.2 conformance, including
      SOAP 1.2 encoding and RPC, the SOAP
      processing model, faults, MTOM/XOP, SwA and swaRef attachments, the HTTP and MIME bindings, WS-Addressing 1.0,
      XML Schema values, message limits and valid data provider examples
    - @ref soapclientintro "SoapClient" @ref soapclientreleasenotes "1.0.4": SOAP 1.2, response header processing,
      MTOM requests, WS-Addressing, SOAP 1.2 \c GET
      retrieval, message and response size limits
    - @ref soaphandlerintro "SoapHandler" @ref soaphandlerreleasenotes "0.3.4": SOAP 1.2, header processing, MTOM
      responses, SwA, WS-Addressing, SOAP 1.2 \c GET
      resources, message limits and conforming HTTP handling
    - @ref soapdataproviderintro "SoapDataProvider" @ref soapdataproviderreleasenotes "2.1": MIME format request
      options, typed request descriptions, search options and
      localized provider catalogs
    - @ref salesforcesoapclientintro "SalesforceSoapClient" @ref salesforcesoapclientreleasenotes "1.5": the
      SoapClient 1.0.4 changes, and a polling connection ping
    - @ref webdavhandlerintro "WebDavHandler" @ref webdavhandlerreleasenotes "1.3": RFC 4918 compliance classes 1 and
      2 with write-lock enforcement and \c If header
      evaluation; passes the litmus compliance suite (see @ref xml230webdav)
    - @ref xmlrpchandlerintro "XmlRpcHandler" @ref xmlrpchandlerreleasenotes "1.1.1": malformed requests always
      receive their XML-RPC fault response
    - @ref xmlrpcconnectionintro "XmlRpcConnection" @ref xmlrpcconnectionreleasenotes "1.6": a polling connection ping
    - @ref saxdataproviderintro "SaxDataProvider" @ref saxdataproviderreleasenotes "1.11": application and action
      registration for reading and writing XML records, and
      localized provider metadata
    - @ref webcontentutilintro "WebContentUtil" @ref webcontentutilreleasenotes "1.0": URL normalization and link
      resolution with the %Qore 3.0 URI resolution

    @section xml220 xml Module Version 2.2.0
    - new @ref webcontentutilintro "WebContentUtil" user module providing reusable web
      content processing primitives consolidated from \c qctl, \c saasctl, and the planned
      \c QorusAiWebCrawler service:
      - @ref WebContentUtil::WebCrawler "WebCrawler" — parallel BFS web crawler built on
        @ref httpclientiointro "HttpClientIo" with HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 multiplexing,
        atomic visited-index dedup, automatic 429/503 throttling, configurable User-Agent
        and max response size, and full SSRF protection
      - @ref WebContentUtil::SsrfFilter "SsrfFilter" — server-side request forgery
        protection rejecting private IPv4 (RFC 1918, loopback, link-local, CGNAT, multicast),
        private IPv6 (ULA, link-local, IPv4-mapped), Kubernetes service DNS, \c .local,
        and cloud metadata endpoints by default
      - @ref WebContentUtil::RobotsTxt "RobotsTxt" — RFC 9309 robots.txt parser with
        \c User-agent group matching, \c Allow / \c Disallow precedence, \c Crawl-delay,
        and \c Sitemap extraction
      - @ref WebContentUtil::UrlNormalizer "UrlNormalizer" — full RFC 3986 URL normalization
        (fragment stripping, path resolution, lowercase scheme/host, default port stripping,
        trailing slash policy, percent-encoding canonicalization, query parameter sorting)
      - @ref WebContentUtil::HtmlExtractor "HtmlExtractor" — HTML to plain text extraction
        with element stripping
      - @ref WebContentUtil::TextChunker "TextChunker" — paragraph/sentence chunking with
        configurable target token size and overlap, suitable for embedding pipelines
      - @ref WebContentUtil::LocalDocReader "LocalDocReader" — recursive local HTML
        directory reader with file type filtering and directory exclusion

    @section xml210 xml Module Version 2.1.0
    <b>Correctness and Robustness Improvements</b>
    - replaced assertions with proper error handling in the @ref Qore::Xml::AbstractXmlIoInputCallback
      "AbstractXmlIoInputCallback" class for production safety
    - added exception checking after container operations in XML parsing to prevent potential issues
      with error propagation
    - added input validation for numeric conversions in XML-RPC parsing with proper error messages for
      invalid or out-of-range integer values
    - improved error handling consistency across the module

    <b>WSDL/SOAP Improvements</b>
    - fixed namespace handling: improved prefix generation (6 chars), fixed asymmetric namespace mapping
    - added @ref WSDL::Namespaces::tryGetInputNamespaceUri "tryGetInputNamespaceUri()" for safe
      namespace lookups without exceptions
    - fixed WSOperation serialize/deserialize methods to properly support header-only operations
    - added @c \@debug blocks for namespace stack operations to aid troubleshooting
    - enabled debug mode in CI tests to catch @c \@debug block parse errors

    <b>Documentation Improvements</b>
    - updated README with modern CMake build instructions and feature overview
    - added more comprehensive usage examples

    @section xml201 xml Module Version 2.0.1
    - @ref wsdlintro "WSDL" module changes:
      - added support for the XSD \c include statement
      - fixed resolving relative \c schemaLocation values; they are now resolved against the location of the document
        containing the reference and not against the location of the top-level WSDL
      - fixed handling element \c ref attributes with no namespace prefix
      - fixed handling element declarations with no type information; such elements now have type \c xs:anyType
      - fixed handling \c simpleType \c union definitions with more than one anonymous member type definition
      - fixed resolving \c simpleType \c restriction base types declared after the restricting type in the schema
      - fixed a bug where namespace prefixes used in serialized messages were not declared in the SOAP envelope when
        a namespace prefix had to be renamed while merging namespaces from an external schema
      - added support for the built-in XSD types derived from \c xs:string (\c xs:NCName, \c xs:ID, \c xs:QName, etc)
      - fixed generating example messages for \c complexType \c choice definitions and \c simpleType \c union
        definitions
      - fixed serializing XSD attribute values; attribute values are now serialized according to their type like
        element values, so dates, booleans, and binary values are written in their XSD representation
      - fixed serializing \c xs:boolean values given as strings; \c "false" and \c "0" are now serialized as
        \c "false" instead of \c "true"
      - fixed handling \c complexType \c choice definitions declared with \c minOccurs="0"; such choices no longer
        require a member to be present
      - fixed a runtime error serializing a \c complexType \c choice with no member supplied; a descriptive
        exception is now thrown if the choice is required
      - fixed a runtime error retrieving the data provider type for a \c complexType \c choice definition
      - fixed deserializing messages whose parts are split between the SOAP header and the SOAP body; the body parts
        are now taken from the \c parts attribute of the \c soap:body element, or, if that attribute is not present,
        from the message parts not bound to a SOAP header
      - fixed reporting enumeration values for numeric, boolean, and date types; they were always reported as strings,
        which also caused an error when retrieving the data provider type for such a type
      - added the missing \c xs:int, \c xs:float, and \c xs:double data provider type mappings; values of these types
        were reported as \c any
      - data provider types for scalar XSD types are now soft types (ex: \c softint), matching the values accepted
        when serializing
      - fixed the data provider type for \c xs:boolean; string values are now parsed according to the XSD boolean
        lexical space, so that \c "false" and \c "0" are converted to @ref False "False"
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5405">issue 5405</a>)
      - fixed handling nested XSD model groups; elements declared in an \c xs:sequence, \c xs:choice, or \c xs:all
        nested in another model group were silently dropped from the type, so they could neither be serialized nor
        deserialized
      - added support for \c xs:choice alternatives declared as nested model groups; all the elements of a single
        alternative can now be serialized and deserialized together
      - fixed the data provider type for a \c complexType with a \c sequence containing a \c choice block; the
        members of the choice were not reported as fields
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5410">issue 5410</a>)
      - added validation of XSD \c pattern facets; \c pattern facets were parsed neither on serialization nor on
        deserialization, so values violating the pattern declared for their type were sent and accepted silently.
        Multiple \c pattern facets declared in the same restriction are combined as if they appeared in a single
        regular expression separated by \c "|" (XSD 1.0 part 2 4.3.4.3), so a value matching any one of them is
        valid; patterns declared in different derivation steps are intersected.  Patterns are anchored as a whole,
        so that a pattern with a top-level alternation such as \c "a|b" does not accept any value merely starting
        with \c "a" or ending with \c "b"
      - added validation of the \c length, \c minLength, \c maxLength, \c minInclusive, \c maxInclusive,
        \c minExclusive, \c maxExclusive, \c totalDigits, and \c fractionDigits facets; only \c enumeration
        was validated before
      - XSD regular expressions are now translated to PCRE before use; the two languages are not the same:
        \c "^" and \c "$" are ordinary characters in XSD, XSD has the \c "\i", \c "\I", \c "\c", and
        \c "\C" escapes and character class subtraction that PCRE does not have, and \c "\s", \c "\w", and
        \c "\d" denote different character sets in the two languages
      - example messages generated by \c WSMessageHelper now satisfy the constraining facets declared for their
        types; previously the placeholder values used for the base types normally violated them, so a generated
        example message could not be serialized
      - added support for the XSD \c "\p{IsXxx}" Unicode block escapes; PCRE has no block support, so such a
        pattern previously failed to compile with a \c REGEX-COMPILATION-ERROR
      - added \c whiteSpace processing; \c whiteSpace is a normalization facet rather than a constraint, so the
        lexical value is now normalized before it is validated.  \c xs:string preserves whitespace,
        \c xs:normalizedString replaces every tab, newline, and carriage return with a space, and \c xs:token
        and every other built-in type additionally collapse runs of spaces and trim the value
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5411">issue 5411</a>)
      - fixed handling \c xs:choice model groups where one of the alternatives can be satisfied by no element at
        all (ex: every member declared with \c minOccurs="0"); such a choice does not require any member to be
        present, but an exception was raised when serializing or deserializing a message with no member of the
        choice
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5464">issue 5464</a>)
      - added support for named model groups (\c xs:group); group references were ignored, so the elements of a
        referenced group could neither be serialized nor deserialized, and a choice with a group alternative
        raised an exception for valid messages
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5464">issue 5464</a>)
      - fixed handling elements whose type is a \c complexType declaring empty content (ex: Ericsson EDA/MTAS
        WSDLs); such types were not parsed at all, so optional elements of such a type were silently dropped when
        serializing, and there was no way to serialize an empty element.  An empty hash now serializes to an
        element with empty content, content supplied for such an element raises an exception instead of being
        dropped silently, such an element is deserialized as an empty hash instead of @ref nothing "NOTHING", and
        its data provider type is a hash type with no fields instead of \c nothing
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5465">issue 5465</a>)
      - a present element with no content whose type has an emptiable content model (ex: a list type) is now
        deserialized as the type's record with no members instead of @ref nothing "NOTHING", independently of
        namespace declarations on the element, and an empty hash supplied for an optional element of such a type
        is serialized as an element with no content instead of being left out of the message
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5465">issue 5465</a>)
      - fixed handling model groups declared with \c minOccurs="0"; their required elements were required even
        when the group was not present; they are now required only when any element of the group is present
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5465">issue 5465</a>)
      - fixed deserializing SOAP messages where a SOAP header part is bound to the same message as the body part and
        the body part has the same name as the message (ex: Ericsson CAI3G WSDLs); the body value was silently
        replaced by the header values; body values are also no longer overwritten by header values or discarded when
        they cannot be merged with the header values
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5453">issue 5453</a>)
    - fixed a memory error in handling I/O info in XML-RPC calls
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4958">issue 4958</a>)

